WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.690
A typical LLM API architecture includes clients, an API gateway or reverse proxy,

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:10.520
an application backend, an inference server, a model runtime, model files, a tokenizer,

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.520
compute resources, queues, caches, logs, monitoring, and storage.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.610
Not every system needs every component on day one,

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:20.740
but each component represents a responsibility that becomes important as the service

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:21.235
grows.

00:00:21.235 --> 00:00:24.400
Requests usually begin in application code or a user-facing client.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.500
The request may pass through a gateway or reverse proxy that handles TLS, routing,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.105
authentication, rate limits, and policy checks.

00:00:31.105 --> 00:00:36.000
The application backend may add business context, retrieve documents, apply authorization,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.860
format messages, or select a model.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.715
The inference server then passes the prepared request to a runtime that loads the model

00:00:41.715 --> 00:00:42.760
and produces output.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.550
The model runtime depends on model files, tokenizer information, CPU resources,

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.833
GPU resources, memory, and configuration.

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.880
Queues can smooth bursts of demand.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.061
Caches may reduce repeated work for embeddings or common requests.

00:00:55.061 --> 00:00:59.450
Logs and metrics provide visibility into latency, errors, token use, queue depth,

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:00.680
and resource pressure.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.635
Storage may hold prompts, outputs, embeddings, configuration,

00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:05.880
or audit records depending on the design.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.965
Good architecture makes request flow visible.

00:01:07.965 --> 00:01:11.460
Teams should be able to explain how a prompt moves from caller to model and how the

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.920
response returns.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.955
They should know where authentication happens, where user authorization is checked,

00:01:15.955 --> 00:01:20.160
where sensitive data may be logged, where timeouts apply, and where failures are reported.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.320
An LLM API is an application service, not a single model file with a port open.
